S("S001","Fix an arbitrary metric space X with its Borel structure, a subset A and a real p. Temporarily assume L1: p>0. M001 applies to this metric space viewed as an extended metric space; its inner nested infima and empty-member supremum are exactly D004. Hence μH[p](A)=sup_{r∈[0,∞],r>0} C_p(r,A).",["M001"],[],["X","A","p","C","μH","ediam"],[F("F001","μH[p](A)=sup_{r∈[0,∞],r>0} C_p(r,A).",["X","A","p","C","μH"],["L1"],[b("r","[0,∞]")])],["L1"]),
S("S002","Any admissible cover bounds the defining infimum from above. For positive scales δ₁≤δ₂, every δ₁-cover is a δ₂-cover, so C_p(δ₂,A)≤C_p(δ₁,A), also when a family of covers is empty. Finite positive real scales are cofinal towards zero among positive extended scales: below a finite r>0 take its real value or half that value, and below r=∞ take 1. Thus every content appearing in F001 is bounded by one at a finite positive scale, while every finite positive scale already occurs there. It follows that μH[p](A)=sup_{δ∈ℝ,δ>0} C_p(δ,A). The same antitonicity shows that for every real sequence δ_k>0 tending to zero, the supremum of its contents equals this supremum: below any fixed δ>0 one eventually has δ_k≤δ.",[],["F001"],["X","A","p","C","μH"],[F("F002","For every δ>0, an admissible δ-cover bounds C_p(δ,A) above; for 0<δ₁≤δ₂, C_p(δ₂,A)≤C_p(δ₁,A). Also μH[p](A)=sup_{δ∈ℝ,δ>0} C_p(δ,A), and any positive real sequence tending to zero has the same supremum of contents.",["X","A","p","C","μH"],["L1"],[b("δ","ℝ"),b("δ₁","ℝ"),b("δ₂","ℝ")])]),
S("S003","For the forward small-cover assertion temporarily suppose L2: μH[p](A)=0. By F002, every finite-scale content is zero. At a fixed positive δ and ε, if no admissible cost were <ε then every admissible cost would be ≥ε and its infimum would be ≥ε; if there were no admissible family the infimum would be ∞. Both contradict zero content. Thus there is a cover of cost <ε for every δ>0, ε>0. Discharge L2 into this implication. Conversely temporarily suppose L3: such covers exist for all δ>0 and ε>0. Each content is then ≤ε for every ε>0, and is nonnegative, so is zero by order density in [0,∞]. F002 gives μH[p](A)=0. Discharge L3. This establishes both directions without assuming a minimizer of an infimum.",[],["F002"],["X","A","p","C","μH"],[F("F003","μH[p](A)=0 iff for every finite δ>0 and ε>0 an admissible ℕ-indexed δ-cover of A has total p-cost <ε.",["X","A","p","C","μH"],["L1"],[b("δ","ℝ"),b("ε","ℝ")])],["L2","L3"],["L2","L3"]),
S("S004","At finite scale every nonempty cover member has finite ediam, equal to the embedding of its real diam. Since p>0, zero diameter has p-power zero; the supremum over a nonempty-member proposition is exactly that diameter power, and for an empty member it is zero. Thus the infimum in D004 is precisely the original ordinary-diameter infimum H^p_δ, and F002 identifies μH[p] with the original H^p with no factor. Any countable subfamily, or the pairs (i,j) with i∈ℕ and 0≤j<N_i for a natural-number family N, has a countable index set: it is a subset of ℕ or ℕ×ℕ. Inject it into ℕ, place each set at its assigned index, and use ∅ elsewhere. The union is unchanged and each nonzero cost occurs once; the sum, defined as the supremum of finite subsums, is unchanged. These conclusions, F002 and F003 hold for all the arbitrary objects. Discharge L1 into p>0 and universally quantify to obtain every clause of N001.",[],["F002","F003"],["X","A","p","ediam","diam","C","μH"])
